  "query_preview": "(This evening through Thursday)...\nIssued at 223 PM EDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nToday and Tonight... \n\nA combination of a strong subgeostrophic upper \njet and weak low pressure over the Atlantic has subdued the \namplitude and strength of a ridge over the SE CONUS, with the upper \njet now dipping into portions of the Ohio Valley. This has been the \nfocus for multiple round of convection over the last 24 hours, \nthe last of which as produced a outflow boundary currently pushing\nthrough central Indiana. Locations south of the boundary will \ncontinue to see typical diurnal curves with temperatures in the \nlow to mid 90s. North of the boundary, temperatures have dropped 5\nto 10 degrees. As the boundary mixes this afternoon, temperatures\nwill attempt to rebound, but likely will still remain at or below\n90. \n\nThis strong outflow boundary has produced a multitude of caveats \nto the forecast leading to fairly high uncertainty in the QPF \nforecast just 12 to 24 hours out. Model guidance has had a \ndifficult time assimilating this boundary, of which will likely \nplay a large factor on where convection is focused late this \nevening through tonight. There is also a broader synoptic front \nthat will near from the NE tonight. This will likely play out in \none of two ways: \n\n    1. The mesoscale boundary and synoptic front are misaligned \nleading to a more diffuse and irregular area of showers and \nthunderstorms. Within this scenario, central Indiana would likely\nget a broad area of 0.5-1.0\" of rain with isolated pockets of \nhigher amounts\n\n    2. The mesoscale boundary and synoptic front align prior to \ninitiation. This would likely result in a more prolific, but narrow \nswath of heavy rain with amounts in the 2-3\" range where the\nboundary settles. Due to this potential scenario, a Flood Watch \nhas been issued from 10PM until 10AM EDT for a majority of East \nCentral Indiana. \n\nFriday through Monday... \n\nAs mentioned, a synoptic boundary will work its way into central \nIndiana tonight into tomorrow, with a large theta-e gradient across \nthe state tomorrow. This gradient will generally be aligned just \nsouth of the I-74 corridor with temperatures in the low 80s to the \nN/E and upper 80s to the S/W. Synoptic forcing will not be \nabundant tomorrow, and the 800-700mb lapse rates look to be rather\nweak. That said, a few isolated showers and storms cannot be \nruled out along the boundary in the afternoon. \n\nThe remainder of the weekend will be a constant push/pull of this \nlingering stationary boundary over the region creating both a swing \nin temperatures and  rain chances. The best chances for rain look\nto be Friday night into Saturday as the front shifts back to the \neast. With additional chances late Saturday through Monday morning\nas weak waves propagate along the boundary. Temperatures are \nexpected to remain warm, with some uncertainty depending on \nboundary placement and cloud cover extent; general expectations \nare for highs in the upper 80s each afternoon for Indianapolis.",
